  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 212 AM MDT Thu Aug 20 2026\n\nUpper-level ridging continues to dominate the long term weather \npattern, with models suggesting the ridge starting to breakdown \nMonday evening into the overnight hours Tuesday. For Saturday, the \nridge will be planted directly overhead with much stronger 500mb \nvorticity maxima ejecting across the CWA. 500mb flow also suggests \nthat monsoonal moisture will start to return to the area Saturday \nafter being largely cutoff by the developing upper-level ridge. \nBehind the weak cold front moving through the region Friday \nafternoon and evening, 700mb temperatures will drop into the 14 to \n16C range with 700mb flow flipping more southerly across the region \nahead of an incoming 700mb shortwave trough. Weaker easterly flow is \nexpected mid-day Saturday before surface pressure gradients start to \nincrease into the afternoon hours, resulting in increasing easterly \nwinds across the region. Surface temperatures will be cooler than \nFriday, in the low-80s to low-90s across the region. Easterly \nsurface flow combined with the return of some monsoonal moisture and \nthe forcing provided by the incoming shortwave trough will help \ninitiation showers and storms along the Laramie Range into the \nevening and overnight hours. Thanks to this returning moisture, \nprecipitable water values will increase to the 97.5th percentile, \njust over an inch for much of the CWA. Therefore, showers and storms \non Saturday may produce heavy rain, which will act to further limit \nfire weather concerns through the weekend.\n\nA slightly more dynamic setup is expected Sunday as the upper-level \nridge continues to push easterly before flattening out by Monday \nafternoon. Westerly to southwesterly flow aloft is anticipated as \nthe ridge advances eastward with multiple 500mb vorticity maxima \npushing through the region. 500mb flow continues to support the \nreturn of monsoonal moisture region with westerly flow developing at \n500b with a fetch all the way to the Pacific Ocean just off the \ncoast of southern California. NAEFS Mean Precipitable Water \ncontinues to suggest PWs in the 97.5th percentile across the region \nSunday, which is favorable for heavy rain across the region. The \n700mb shortwave trough will continue to push easterly Sunday morning \ninto the evening hours, taking with it the best forcing. A 700mb \nridge starts to build off to the south and west after the shortwave \npushes out of the region with westerly flow developing across the \nCWA. Isolated to scattered showers and storms may develop mid-day \nand continue through the evening hours Sunday, despite ridging \nremaining aloft and the best forcing well off to the east. Sunday \nwill be warmer than Saturday as 700mb temperatures increase until \nthe advancing ridge, with highs expected to be in the mid-80s to mid-\n90s once more.\n\nA brief period of zonal flow aloft develops for Tuesday and most of \nWednesday before another ridge begins to build across the western \nCONUS starting Wednesday and continuing through the end of the week. \n700mb flow remains largely calm during the week. Several 500mb \nvorticity maxima will push across the region as the monsoonal \nmoisture fetch remains open across the region. Daily shower and \nstorm potential is expected Monday through much of the week, with \nheavy rain possible each day. Temperatures will be warm for most of \nthe week, but not overly hot. Daily high temperatures will be in the \nlow- to upper-80s through Wednesday. As the ridge begins to build \nback over the region Wednesday and Thursday, temperatures start to \nincrease with high temperatures in the mid-80s to mid-90s expected \nto return by Thursday.",
